{
 "fps": 30,
 "totalFrames": 1733,
 "lines": [
  {
   "i": 0,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "The night Abraham Lincoln was shot, the policeman guarding his door wasn't there.",
   "from": 0,
   "to": 110
  },
  {
   "i": 1,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Washington, April fourteenth, eighteen sixty-five. The war is all but over. Lincoln is at Ford's Theatre, watching a comedy from a private box.",
   "from": 113,
   "to": 336
  },
  {
   "i": 2,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "His guard: police officer John Parker. At intermission, Parker reportedly heads to the saloon next door.",
   "from": 340,
   "to": 461
  },
  {
   "i": 3,
   "speaker": "barman",
   "text": "What'll it be, officer?",
   "from": 464,
   "to": 521
  },
  {
   "i": 4,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Nobody knows if he came back. He wasn't at the door.",
   "from": 524,
   "to": 594
  },
  {
   "i": 5,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Three days earlier, Lincoln had backed voting rights for some Black men. Famous actor John Wilkes Booth was reportedly in the crowd.",
   "from": 598,
   "to": 744
  },
  {
   "i": 6,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Just after ten, Booth climbs the stairs. He shows the footman a card, slips into the passage, and bars the door with a wooden plank.",
   "from": 748,
   "to": 924
  },
  {
   "i": 7,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Then he waits for the biggest laugh.",
   "from": 928,
   "to": 970
  },
  {
   "i": 8,
   "speaker": "actor",
   "text": "You sockdologizing old man-trap!",
   "from": 974,
   "to": 1042
  },
  {
   "i": 9,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "One shot, hidden in the laughter.",
   "from": 1046,
   "to": 1082
  },
  {
   "i": 10,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Lincoln dies the next morning.",
   "from": 1085,
   "to": 1120
  },
  {
   "i": 11,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Now, what if Parker had stayed? Maybe Booth is turned away. Maybe he tries another night.",
   "from": 1123,
   "to": 1271
  },
  {
   "i": 12,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "Instead, Andrew Johnson took over. He pardoned thousands of Confederates and vetoed bills protecting freed slaves. Many historians think Lincoln would have protected those rights better. Others say Congress mattered more.",
   "from": 1274,
   "to": 1557
  },
  {
   "i": 13,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "And Parker? Charged with neglect of duty. The charge was dropped.",
   "from": 1561,
   "to": 1645
  },
  {
   "i": 14,
   "speaker": "narrator",
   "text": "One empty chair, and a country took a different road.",
   "from": 1649,
   "to": 1709
  }
 ],
